FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/lib.rs:157-163 — The explicit-path branch `rb test <file>` has no zero-execution guard: `harness.run_file(p)?` followed by `PrettyReporter::report` returns `Ok(())` when the file defines no `test` blocks (the reporter only exits when `failed > 0`, src/testing/reporter.rs:119-121). Bare `rb test` now rejects this exact case (src/lib.rs:189-200) on the stated principle "A run that executed nothing is not a passing suite", but `rb test empty.rb` still exits 0 having verified nothing, so CI invoking the file form gets a false green. — Fix: apply the same `results.total == 0` rejection to the `Some` branch (or share one helper between both). Traced by inspection; `edge_rb_test_that_defines_no_tests_fails` (tests/discovery_test.rs:919-946) exercises only the project form, so nothing pins the file form as green.

2. [MINOR] src/testing/mod.rs:68-71 — When `run_tests_in` catches a per-file `run_file` error it calls `harness.add_error`, and that helper hardcodes `file: "unknown"` / `line: None` (src/testing/harness.rs:257-265). A project whose test file is unreadable (e.g. non-UTF-8, tests/discovery_test.rs:331-349) therefore reports `Location: unknown`, undoing the phase's "failures name the file" improvement. — Fix: record the path on this path too (a helper like `add_source_error` that takes the path), instead of the anonymous `add_error`.

3. [MINOR] src/testing/mod.rs:104 — `if depth > MAX_TEST_DIR_DEPTH` allows depths 0..=64, i.e. 65 nested directories below the root, while the constant/doc say "the most directory levels … is 64". The check also runs on entry before `read_dir`, so the actual cut-off is one level later than documented. — Fix: use `depth >= MAX_TEST_DIR_DEPTH` or adjust the doc/message to the real bound; the test only asserts the message contains "levels deep" (tests/discovery_test.rs:145-150), so it does not pin the exact limit.

4. [MINOR] tests/discovery_test.rs:625-626 — The scaffold test only asserts that `src/main.rb` exists; it never parses or runs it. `rb new` writes `say "Hello from {name}"` (src/project.rs:135-138), and a malformed generated entry point would leave the gate green. — Fix: run the generated entry (e.g. `rb run src/main.rb` and assert it succeeds/prints "Hello from hello"), or parse it through the lexer/parser in the assertion.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 4
